22. Emergence of Development Economics and Development in Historical Perspective
Paper: Economics of Growth and Development I (ECNCC302) | Unit: I | Topic: Emergence of development economics as a discipline; development in historical perspective
Introduction
Development economics is the branch of economics that studies why some countries are rich and others poor, and how poor countries can grow and improve living standards. It emerged as a distinct discipline after World War II.
Emergence as a discipline
• Classical roots: Adam Smith (division of labour, markets), Ricardo (comparative advantage, land constraint), Malthus (population), Marx (capital accumulation) and Schumpeter (innovation) were concerned with growth long before development economics existed.
• Post-war context: decolonisation created many new poor nations; the Marshall Plan's success raised hopes of planned development; the Cold War brought aid; and the Great Depression discredited laissez-faire.
• Founding contributions (1940s-50s): Rosenstein-Rodan (Big Push, 1943), Nurkse (balanced growth, vicious circle), Lewis (dual economy, 1954), Myrdal and Hirschman (unbalanced growth), Prebisch and Singer (terms of trade), Rostow (stages of growth).
• Distinct features: structural change, market failures, institutions, and an active role for the state. Early development economists argued that standard neoclassical theory did not apply in poor countries.
Phases of thinking
1. 1950s-60s: structuralism, with industrialisation, planning, import substitution and capital accumulation.
2. 1970s: redistribution with growth, basic needs and employment.
3. 1980s: neoclassical counterrevolution, with the Washington Consensus, stabilisation and structural adjustment.
4. 1990s-2000s: institutions and human development, with Sen's capabilities, endogenous growth, governance, and microfinance.
5. Today: randomised experiments, sustainability (SDGs), and global value chains.
Development in historical perspective
Before 1800, income differences between regions were modest. The Industrial Revolution produced the "Great Divergence": Western Europe and its offshoots took off while Asia, Africa and Latin America lagged. Acemoglu and Robinson stress colonial institutions, while Landes and Pomeranz emphasise culture and geography/resources respectively. Latecomers like Japan, East Asian Tigers and China caught up through state-guided industrialisation, while others suffered from colonial extraction and unequal terms of trade.
Conclusion
Development economics arose from the conviction that poor countries face distinct structural problems; its evolution reflects changing views on the balance between markets, states and institutions.